# <Your Topic Title> — authoritative sources

<!--
This file is the ground truth your research is measured against. If you use
`tools/new-topic`, it generates this for you from a brief (see docs/topic-authoring.md);
this file shows the shape if you'd rather write one by hand.

AUTHORITY.md and TOPIC.md are operator-owned: a research agent may read them but must
never rewrite scope, obligations, or the exit condition here. If you edit either file
after approval, run `chassis/semantic-state.py rehash <topic-dir>` afterward -- that's
the explicit "yes, I changed the scope" action; nothing does it automatically.
-->

## Mission and boundaries

One or two sentences: what question is this topic actually answering, and for whom?
Then name what's explicitly out of scope -- the fastest way to keep a topic finite is
to say what it is NOT about.

## Scope (source material for obligations)

Write your scope as a list of distinct points, separated by semicolons or as separate
sentences -- this is what `tools/new-topic` decomposes into individual obligations, and
it's also just clearer to read. Each point should be checkable: something a piece of
research can actually confirm, contradict, or admit is unresolved. Avoid vague framing
like "explore X" with no way to know when you've explored enough of it.

## Evidence-quality vocabulary

The default T1-T4 tier scaffold (see CONTRACT-CORE.md) is a reasonable starting point,
but replace it if your domain has better-fitting quality dimensions -- e.g. peer-review
status and sample size for a scientific claim, or documented-platform-behavior vs.
vendor-marketing for a product-evaluation topic like the SEO/AEO or ATS-parsing kind of
question. The chassis never enforces one universal hierarchy; this file does.

## Required comparisons / counterevidence duties (optional)

If your topic needs specific comparisons (e.g. "compare X against Y on Z"), or has a
known failure mode you want explicitly guarded against (e.g. "actively look for
evidence that contradicts the popular claim about X"), name it here. This is exactly
the kind of thing that's easy to lose if it's only implied by the mission statement.
